WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.198
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.498 --> 00:00:10.151
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.451 --> 00:00:17.701
Unit seven: Comparative Literature. Today you will compare and contrast with whereas, much as and the more… the more.

4
00:00:18.001 --> 00:00:20.584
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.533 --> 00:00:25.149
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.983 --> 00:00:27.844
Cross-cultural analysis.

7
00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:33.473
Cross-cultural analysis means the study of how ideas appear in different cultures.

8
00:00:34.083 --> 00:00:37.523
Cross-cultural analysis compared two creation myths.

9
00:00:38.117 --> 00:00:42.000
Your turn. Say: Cross-cultural analysis.

10
00:00:44.833 --> 00:00:46.682
Cross-cultural analysis.

11
00:00:47.417 --> 00:00:48.859
Translated text.

12
00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:54.891
Translated text means a work rewritten in a language other than its original one.

13
00:00:55.483 --> 00:00:58.528
A translated text always involves choices.

14
00:00:59.117 --> 00:01:02.175
Your turn. Say: Translated text.

15
00:01:05.033 --> 00:01:06.464
Translated text.

16
00:01:07.217 --> 00:01:08.612
Literary tradition.

17
00:01:09.617 --> 00:01:14.797
Literary tradition means a long-running set of styles and forms in a culture's writing.

18
00:01:15.383 --> 00:01:18.091
The epic belongs to an ancient literary tradition.

19
00:01:18.683 --> 00:01:22.021
Your turn. Say: Literary tradition.

20
00:01:24.867 --> 00:01:26.112
Literary tradition.

21
00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:28.211
Comparative method.

22
00:01:29.217 --> 00:01:33.608
Comparative method means a way of learning by placing two things side by side.

23
00:01:34.217 --> 00:01:36.657
The comparative method reveals patterns.

24
00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:40.425
Your turn. Say: Comparative method.

25
00:01:43.267 --> 00:01:44.813
Comparative method.

26
00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:46.876
World literature.

27
00:01:47.883 --> 00:01:52.019
World literature means writing from many cultures that is read across borders.

28
00:01:52.617 --> 00:01:55.046
World literature reaches readers everywhere.

29
00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:58.430
Your turn. Say: World literature.

30
00:02:01.267 --> 00:02:02.697
World literature.

31
00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:04.939
Textual comparison.

32
00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:10.028
Textual comparison means close study of the words of two texts together.

33
00:02:10.617 --> 00:02:13.487
Textual comparison showed similar images.

34
00:02:14.083 --> 00:02:17.107
Your turn. Say: Textual comparison.

35
00:02:19.967 --> 00:02:21.699
Textual comparison.

36
00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:24.008
Cultural context.

37
00:02:25.017 --> 00:02:28.665
Cultural context means the beliefs and customs that surround a work.

38
00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:32.016
Cultural context explains the hero's duty.

39
00:02:32.617 --> 00:02:35.815
Your turn. Say: Cultural context.

40
00:02:38.667 --> 00:02:40.318
Cultural context.

41
00:02:41.083 --> 00:02:42.444
Universal theme.

42
00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:47.342
Universal theme means an idea found in the literature of all cultures.

43
00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:50.368
Love and loss are universal themes.

44
00:02:50.983 --> 00:02:54.181
Your turn. Say: Universal theme.

45
00:02:57.033 --> 00:02:58.336
Universal theme.

46
00:02:59.133 --> 00:03:02.877
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:03.717 --> 00:03:09.689
Visal, a critic writes 'The more we compare the epics, the more we notice shared fears.' What is the pattern?

48
00:03:10.783 --> 00:03:21.879
The double comparative: the more… the more. It shows two changes that grow together. The more translations we read, the less we trust a single version.

49
00:03:22.983 --> 00:03:24.890
How do we contrast two works?

50
00:03:25.983 --> 00:03:39.909
Use whereas or while: 'The Greek epic stresses glory, whereas the Khmer tale stresses duty.' Much as means in the same way as, but with a small difference: Much as I admire it, I disagree.

51
00:03:41.017 --> 00:03:42.807
Is there a common mistake?

52
00:03:43.917 --> 00:03:56.231
Learners forget the in the double comparative. Say the more, the less, not more… more. And keep each part in comparative form: the longer the poem, the harder the translation.

53
00:03:57.367 --> 00:03:59.215
Part three: grammar.

54
00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:04.383
Grammar focus: Comparing: the more… the more and whereas and much as.

55
00:04:04.683 --> 00:04:05.916
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:06.617 --> 00:04:10.334
The more we compare the epics, the more shared fears we notice.

57
00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:15.537
The Greek epic stresses glory, whereas the Khmer tale stresses duty.

58
00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:19.529
The longer the poem, the harder the translation becomes.

59
00:04:20.467 --> 00:04:27.844
The + comparative, the + comparative shows linked change. Use whereas / while to contrast two facts and much as for a partly shared view.

60
00:04:28.583 --> 00:04:32.141
Be careful! Don't say: More you read, more you learn.

61
00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:36.576
Say: The more you read, the more you learn.

62
00:04:36.876 --> 00:04:38.829
Both parts of the pattern need the.

63
00:04:39.633 --> 00:04:42.332
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:43.133 --> 00:04:44.889
Reading Across Cultures.

65
00:04:45.189 --> 00:04:49.975
Cross-cultural analysis places works from different literary traditions side by side.

66
00:04:50.967 --> 00:04:57.401
The more carefully critics read a translated text, the more they realise how many choices a translator makes.

67
00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:03.616
One tradition may value personal glory, whereas another values duty to the community.

68
00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:10.175
Textual comparison depends on cultural context, and the wider the context, the clearer the meaning.

69
00:05:11.167 --> 00:05:17.079
World literature endures because each universal theme, such as love or loss, speaks to every reader.

70
00:05:18.167 --> 00:05:22.087
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:25.042
The blank you read, the more you learn.

72
00:05:25.342 --> 00:05:28.404
A, more. B, most. C, much.

73
00:05:31.967 --> 00:05:33.467
The answer is A: more.

74
00:05:34.617 --> 00:05:37.661
A work rewritten in another language is a blank text.

75
00:05:37.961 --> 00:05:41.998
A, comparative. B, translated. C, universal.

76
00:05:45.533 --> 00:05:47.800
The answer is B: translated.

77
00:05:48.950 --> 00:05:51.031
Which sentence contrasts correctly?

78
00:05:51.331 --> 00:06:00.418
A, The first rhymes, whereas the second does not.. B, The first rhymes, whereas not rhymes the second.. C, The first whereas rhymes, the second not..

79
00:06:03.967 --> 00:06:07.835
The answer is A: The first rhymes, whereas the second does not..

80
00:06:08.983 --> 00:06:12.539
An idea found in literature of all cultures is a blank theme.

81
00:06:12.839 --> 00:06:16.074
A, local. B, formal. C, universal.

82
00:06:19.633 --> 00:06:22.109
The answer is C: universal.

83
00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:26.863
According to the reading, what endures because of universal themes?

84
00:06:27.163 --> 00:06:31.931
A, world literature. B, one translation. C, a single epic.

85
00:06:35.467 --> 00:06:37.907
The answer is A: world literature.

86
00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:42.402
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:47.374
What can comparing literature from two different cultures teach us about both?

88
00:06:56.183 --> 00:06:57.672
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:57.972 --> 00:07:22.581
Comparing two literatures shows what is shared and what is special. We may see a universal theme, such as grief, treated in very different ways, and we learn that each culture has its own values and habits of storytelling. The more we compare, the better we understand our own tradition as well, because differences make familiar ideas visible.

90
00:07:22.881 --> 00:07:24.869
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:25.783 --> 00:07:31.965
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and comparing: the more… the more and whereas and much as.

92
00:07:32.265 --> 00:07:37.832
Today's value is cultural openness. Read beyond your own tradition to see the world more fully.

93
00:07:38.132 --> 00:07:40.563
See you in Unit eight. Goodbye!

94
00:07:40.863 --> 00:07:43.283
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

